/*****************************************************************************
    countScratch -- the fixture for the KANT `countRuleTerms` (incant/genCount).

    WHY IT DOES NOT GO THROUGH genParse. The C++ fork in countRuleTerms does
    not exist yet (it is foreman's; round 3's report carries the exact diff),
    so `genParse('ScafC')` still runs the C++ body. This fixture therefore
    drives `countTerms` DIRECTLY, on real rule nodes.

    ⚠ THE ORACLE IS THE C++ ITSELF, COMPUTED IN THE SAME RUN, AND THAT IS THE
    WHOLE DESIGN. `dumpRuleTerms` (genParse.rtn:170) prints one `[i] tag` line
    per list entry and a `noPrint (SKIPPED by the walk)` line for each entry
    the C++ walk skips -- so

        expected = (count of "[i] " lines) - (count of "SKIPPED" lines)

    IS the C++ `countRuleTerms` answer, derived rather than transcribed. No
    `.target` file, nothing to regenerate green, and a change to EITHER
    implementation breaks the check (standing rule H3). The kant answer is
    printed as `KOUNT <tag>=<n>` where n is the count PLUS ONE -- so a run in
    which the bias silently disappeared fails every row rather than passing.

    THE CROSS-CHECK, run from the Groups directory. It asserts the ROW COUNT
    as well as the values, so a census that silently lost rules cannot pass by
    comparing fewer things (standing rule H4's other half -- an anti-vacuity
    guard, because a check that compares nothing to nothing is an absence check
    wearing a diff's clothes):

        B=${INCANT:-$HOME/bin/incant}
        $B incant/countScratch > /tmp/cs.out 2> /tmp/cs.err;  echo "EXIT=$?"
        grep -q 'CS SENTINEL' /tmp/cs.out || echo "TRUNCATED"
        awk '/^RULE /{r=$2;t[r]=0;s[r]=0;n++}
             /^    \[/{t[r]++}
             /noPrint \(SKIPPED/{s[r]++}
             /nextGroup: ERROR/{leaf++}
             /^KOUNT /{split($2,a,"=");k[a[1]]=a[2]}
             END{bad=0
                 for(r in t){ok=((r in k)&&(t[r]-s[r]==k[r]-1))
                     printf "%-12s cpp=%d kant=%s %s\n",
                       r,t[r]-s[r],(r in k)?k[r]-1:"NONE",ok?"ok":"MISMATCH"
                     if(!ok)bad++}
                 printf "ROWS=%d MISMATCHES=%d LEAFWALKS=%d\n",n,bad,leaf+0
                 if(n!=23||bad||leaf)print "CROSS-CHECK FAILED"}' /tmp/cs.err

    ⚠ `LEAFWALKS` IS NOT DECORATION AND IT IS PRINTED UNCONDITIONALLY. MEASURED:
    deleting `if argument.listLengtH;` from `incant/genCount` leaves ROWS=23
    MISMATCHES=0 and exit 0 -- the COUNT is unaffected, because a list-less rule
    counts 0 either way. What changes is one `nextGroup: ERROR Modifier does not
    contain a list` line on STDERR, which is where genParse writes its EMITTED
    TEXT. So the guard's whole job is invisible to a count comparison, and the
    check has to name the quantity: LEAFWALKS is the number of list-less walks
    the run performed, printed every time and asserted == 0. Written this way
    round rather than as "assert the message is absent" per standing rule H4 --
    an absence check goes green the day the message is removed. Same shape as
    the JIT degrade counter.

    ⚠ FOUR RULES ARE DELIBERATELY ABSENT FROM THE CENSUS, and the reason is a
    FIXTURE limitation rather than a counter one -- it is named here so nobody
    reads the gap as coverage. This fixture hands `countTerms` a rule by BARE
    NAME, and two things can happen to a bare name that cannot happen to the
    node a C++ caller already holds:

        NumbeR, Attributes, NewGroup   carry `rule.data=isGROUP`, so the name
                                       DEREFERENCES to gGroup -- `NumbeR`
                                       arrives as `numberSet`, and both
                                       `Attributes` and `NewGroup` arrive as
                                       `TraiT`. MEASURED: `KOUNT numberSet=1`
                                       and `KOUNT TraiT=5` twice over.
        CodE                           is SHADOWED -- JSONfield's noPrint
                                       code-tail contains a leaf named `CodE`,
                                       and the bare name finds that instead of
                                       the grammar rule (kant said 0 terms, the
                                       C++ rule has 2).

    The C++ `countRuleTerms` takes a NODE from `planRule`/`parseRuleMethod`,
    which never goes through name resolution, so the seam is unaffected. These
    four are excluded because the FIXTURE cannot address them, not because the
    counter cannot count them.

    ⚠ TAKE `$?` DIRECTLY FROM THE BINARY, NEVER THROUGH A PIPE -- this
    project's shell is zsh and `${PIPESTATUS[0]}` expands to NOTHING there, so
    every run reads as passing (CLAIM KANT-24/PIPESTATUS).

    ⚠ THE JSON PARSE ON LINE `testJSON(...)` IS LOAD-BEARING AND MUST STAY
    FIRST. Parts of a rule's list are materialised LAZILY: before any parse has
    run, JSONfield has SEVEN entries and after one it has EIGHT (the extra is
    the action body's `BlocK`). Both implementations agree either way -- the
    late arrival is noPrint, so the REAL count is 4 before and after -- but a
    census taken on a never-parsed tree exercises fewer noPrint entries than
    the ladder actually meets, which is the same reason termScratch and
    censusScratch both parse first.

    THE ROWS: 23 of the census termScratch/censusScratch use, which is the only
    set that reaches the noPrint tail at all -- only JSONfield (4) and JSONarray
    (3) carry noPrint entries, and a census restricted to the Scaf ladder would
    never discriminate `if !term.noPrinT` from `if 1`. `Modifier` is the row
    that covers the OTHER edge: it has no list at all, so it is the only
    exercise of the `if argument.listLengtH;` guard, and without it a removed
    guard would still read green everywhere.

    SENTINEL AT THE FOOT, checked first and by name. An incant parse failure
    abandons the rest of the file and STILL EXITS 0, so a truncated run is
    otherwise indistinguishable from a short successful one.

    RUN:   <binary> incant/countScratch
*****************************************************************************/
registry(cOMMANDs);
define dumpRuleTerms immediateAction=dumpRuleTerms; ;

registry(fILEs);
define genCount File='incant/genCount'; ;
include(genCount);

register(Ladder);
define
    Scaf  isRule "x"-;
    Scaf2 isRule "{"- "}"-;
    ScafA isRule "a"-;
    ScafB isRule ScafA "b"-;
    ;
search reset stack Grokking Counters Ladder UnitTests Utilities list;

define
    testJSON code={
        clear(listed);
        field = JSONblock(argument);
        };
    /*  ⚠ ONE ACTION PER PRINTED ROW, and `$` is the reason. It toggles ONE
        GLOBAL that is reset to true at ACTION ENTRY and NOT restored on return
        (CLAIM KANT-27), so entering `report` afresh is what makes the row's own
        spelling independent of whatever `countTerms` last left behind.
        Presence-with-value, per standing rule H4: the count is printed
        unconditionally and its VALUE compared, never its absence.  */
    report code={
        n = countTerms(argument);
        cerr $"KOUNT " argument.taG "=" n:;
        };
    census code={
        report(Scaf);
        report(Scaf2);
        report(ScafA);
        report(ScafB);
        report(JSONblock);
        report(JSONfield);
        report(JSONvalue);
        report(JSONtoken);
        report(JSONitem);
        report(JSONlist);
        report(JSONarray);
        report(FloaT);
        report(PoweR);
        report(QuotE);
        report(NamE);
        report(GrouP);
        report(DatA);
        report(Limit);
        report(MemberS);
        report(Modifier);
        report(TraiT);
        report(TraiTdata);
        report(DefinE);
        };
    ;

testJSON('{"a":[1,2]}');

/*  THE C++ SIDE OF THE ORACLE, taken FIRST so a crash in the kant half cannot
